The iron's top face was the one large in-scope gap: co-masked, our texture carried 0.28–0.33 of the reference's amplitude, and at the metric's own Sobel threshold 0.20% edge pixels against 3.46% — that face holds 9.14% of the reference's whole metric-edge population. `relief_filter` had sat in `build_icon.py` unemitted since round 8.

I built it, matched every measured statistic — bands 0.0160/0.0108/0.0077/0.0039 against 0.0214/0.0100/0.0087/0.0080, skew +0.88, face mean +0.0004 — and then looked at a 3× matched crop: **stucco beside slate.** Statistics don't identify a material. The reference is a cleaved plane with hairline scratches; a lit height field embosses and can't make a hairline. So the relief was cut to anisotropic drift and the fine band given to 30 strokes, count set by counting the reference.

Rubric intact: `mask_iou` unmoved, 16px `self_contrast` rose to 0.6141, `edge_f1` at 16 still 1.0, polarity +0.151. The rejection is SSIM at every size — uncorrelated detail, as r02 found and a pre-authoring simulation predicted.

Reusable and confirmed: mean-preserving noise-relief with the transfer's mean-lift folded into `k1`; and grain is a height field, scratches are strokes.

